#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e61d3403-80d9-5633-b579-dcf452dbc265" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/id #uuid "493f9465-dfd4-5103-8aba-e02d92282172" :question/math-academy-id "e-12674" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3, n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template outlined below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case ![](images/55/55b1aa9ddb93f56c2237d8c47d8c4e74126c4f287690fa23f2142a4e926a77c6.svg)\n![](images/f4/f4a96cfa3b106bf988ce2c42346c1654863fe23d0e2db674e4273145c70d2bd2.svg)\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some ![](images/d1/d173450199d3b813b8260082f81b4030333e9e1b0e1949b8972c95a17428162b.svg) We have\n![](images/5b/5b2ed3cdec9017722c5123b54514d3f08787950c90d5b9e8993f45bd0f5fcbe2.svg)\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/85/858c9bbe6b754ef2f70be8a99babad3df452c10cb824f30fded9aa9b8b05189f.svg)\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n > 2n\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 3,$ the base case is $P(3).$ So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}n=3}.$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^3} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2(3)}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n = k \\geq 3.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^k}  > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2k}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^{k+1}} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2(k+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b83c640f-c7fd-5adc-94b8-b129d357b140" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Polynomial Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "6b8b1014-56bf-51c0-be9e-a194181a0d99" :question/math-academy-id "e-13225" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that $n^2 > 2n+2$ for all $n \\geq 3, n\\in \\mathbb N.$" :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nn^2 > 2n+2 \\quad\\text{for}\\quad n \\geq 3.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 3,$ the base case is $P(3).$ So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)+2=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 3.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 3,$ we have\n\n$$\nk^2 > 2k+2.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^2 > 2(k+1)+2 . \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2} & ={k}^{2}+2k+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+2k+1 & ={{({k}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{>2k+2}+2k+1 \\\\  & >2k+2+2k+1 \\\\  & =2k+2+{{2k}_{⏟}}_{>1}+1 \\\\  & >2(k+1)+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2 > 2n+2$ for all $n \\geq 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82830730-a9bc-57bd-9114-d0d50473ea63" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Other Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "f5d8d9e3-d45c-5ad9-aae7-7933e6f4fade" :question/math-academy-id "e-13227" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for $n\\in\\mathbb N.$" :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n +1 \\leq 3^n \\quad\\text{for} \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n = 1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n = 1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1}+1=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1}=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n = 1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2^k +1 \\leq 3^k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n2^{k+1} + 1\\leq 3^{k+1}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k+1}+1 & ={2}^{k}\\cdot {2}^{1}+1 \\\\  & =2({2}^{k})+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{k}+{2}^{k}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k}+{{{2}^{k}+1}_{⏟}}_{\\le {3}^{k}} & \\le {2}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={{{2}^{k}}_{⏟}}_{<{3}^{k}<2\\cdot {3}^{k}}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & <2\\cdot {3}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{k} \\\\  & ={3}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is less than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for all $n \\geq 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b83c640f-c7fd-5adc-94b8-b129d357b140" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Polynomial Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c396347-72fc-57f0-8f59-176c7a17e50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418784} {:answer/id #uuid "e9963c68-ccf4-5183-94e2-250f10549fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9963c68-ccf4-5183-94e2-250f10549fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418923} :answer-field/id #uuid "1216ef77-e331-5d2d-a6bc-ad5097411412" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0248bc7-e5ec-5c3d-aace-8e360631c72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604