/id #edb/ref 17592186418865} {:answer/id #uuid "dbda601d-82c1-5250-8339-40937c92ea29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{k+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aaff72c-0d52-5092-b2f8-ab29dfaa6b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{k+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418865} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8cf8de4-e854-53cc-9c91-3519c91f8f97" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418979} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b789cdc-9c5a-5b92-ac14-c15783d43de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418782} {:answer/id #uuid "9da1a00f-0ce7-5ef9-bd4b-322d6ed09996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2k+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418868} {:answer/id #uuid "b32305ae-be7b-522f-bc0c-bedb31cde651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b32305ae-be7b-522f-bc0c-bedb31cde651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418884} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfa77cfa-58e4-5b25-ae91-85c90631ea73" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22c3e851-1c76-57c4-8e64-d220300f1c79" :question/math-academy-id "q-183366" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-9}} & ={{field-10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {2}^{k+1} & >{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & >{{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{2}^{n+1}>n+1\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1+1}=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+1}>k+1.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+2}>k+2.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{(k+1)+1} & ={2}^{k+1}\\cdot {2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {2}^{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {{{2}^{k+1}}_{⏟}}_{>\\,k+1} & >2(k+1) \\\\  & =2k+2 \\\\  & =k+2+{{k}_{⏟}}_{>\\,0} \\\\  & >k+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e61d3403-80d9-5633-b579-dcf452dbc265" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086469b0-50ff-592d-907c-51a0b13d0499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413872} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ddea05-7746-515f-b8aa-b091bae2ef2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{2}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413914} {:answer/id #uuid "bae5a540-1a9e-503e-8aae-363834bc24c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{2}^{k+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413917} {:answer/id #uuid "f8faf1d4-2303-5a10-8a37-a1a5a7b45e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ddea05-7746-515f-b8aa-b091bae2ef2d" :answer/type {:db/id