 17592186413895} {:answer/id #uuid "9345904f-fd95-5cc5-89ec-58107dde07f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413905} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5068a6-d63e-5c22-9b8a-9536b034107a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{3}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7048b8a2-56f1-51a8-a56e-2712075c0116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{2}^{k+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413894} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2fdff5c-fcbf-5831-9983-a82bbba778f0" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f75ec7c6-91c3-5a79-95d1-5c3dc1cb4b13" :question/math-academy-id "q-183370" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case {{field-1}}.\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}>{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$\n{{field-4}},\nwe have\n\n$$\n{{field-5}}>{{field-6}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n\\ge 2,$ the base case is $P(2).$ So, we substitute $n=2$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n{3}^{2}>1+{2}^{2}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 2.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{k}>1+{2}^{k}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{3}^{k+1}>1+{2}^{k+1}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82830730-a9bc-57bd-9114-d0d50473ea63" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Other Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215ee801-d946-5aef-ab70-32fd58f35dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "less than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418793} {:answer/id #uuid "b892634c-8fb4-5285-a9fb-27a5443f862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}